 (93, 'Tea from Assam – Discuss; Thinking about Language', [
  'Discuss: Pranjol and Rajvir travel by train to Assam (Pranjol\'s father manages the Dhekiabari Tea Estate). Rajvir is delighted by the sea of tea bushes. Legends: Chinese emperor Shen Nung – leaves fell into his boiled water; Bodhidharma cut off his eyelids – ten tea plants grew. Tea was first discovered in Assam in 1823. Second flush (May–July) yields the best tea.',
  'Language I.1: (i) downpour (ii) undergo (iii) upkeep (iv) dropout (v) walk-in',
  'Language I.2: (i) overthrow (ii) breakthrough (iii) bypass (iv) outlook (v) downcast']),
 (94, '-ing / -ed adjectives', [
  '(i) interesting/exciting (ii) interesting/stimulating (iii) bored/depressed (iv) excited/surprised (v) delighted/relaxed (vi) annoyed/irritated/tired (vii) thrilled/excited (viii) interesting/touching']),
 (97, 'The Trees – Thinking about the Poem', [
  '1) (i) In a treeless forest: no bird could sit, no insect could hide, the sun could not bury its feet in shadow. (ii) It creates the picture of sunlight falling on the forest floor covered with shadows of trees; the sun\'s "feet" are its rays touching the ground.',
  '2) (i) The trees are inside the house (in pots on the veranda). Roots work all night to disengage themselves from the cracks in the veranda floor; leaves strain toward the glass; small twigs stiff with exertion. (ii) The poet compares the branches to newly discharged patients going to the clinic doors, half-dazed.',
  '3) (i) (a) At the beginning, the moon is full and shining in the open sky. (b) At the end, the moon is broken like a mirror into pieces and shines in the crown of the tallest oak – because the trees moved out and their branches broke the moonlight. (ii) The house becomes empty and silent; the glass breaks; the smell of leaves and lichen reaches the rooms like a voice. (iii) Perhaps we do not notice or do not want to admit the changes in nature that embarrass us.']),
 (98, 'The Trees – Meaning of the poem', [
  '4) (i) Yes, it presents a conflict between man and nature: man has confined trees inside houses; they feel imprisoned and break out to the forest, their natural home. (ii) Trees may also symbolise women who are confined to the house and want to break free and find their own identity.',
  '5) "On Killing a Tree" (Gieve Patel) – both poems speak of the strength of trees; in Rich\'s poem trees escape, in Patel\'s a tree resists being killed.'])
